{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_93","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '15,80p' docs/testing.md && cat docs/online-tests.md && ls -lh ../*.html ../exit-interview.json\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_93","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '15,80p' docs/testing.md && cat docs/online-tests.md && ls -lh ../*.html ../exit-interview.json\"","aggregated_output":"## Defects found and fixed\n\nTwelve distinct defects were identified through checks and review. Test-fixture mistakes (wrong control label, ambiguous Back selector, insufficient wait for the transport’s end notification, and an unstable10Hz test pilot) are excluded from this count.\n\n| # | Fixed defect | Evidence / regression |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|1|TV scaling clipped title/results actions.|Actual screenshots and title-action visibility assertion; native modal height constrained to logical viewport.|\n|2|Immediate result overlays hid the collapse.|Packaged campaign verifies a2.2s collapse before results; campaign-collapse/victory images.|\n|3|A host finish could overtake the final snapshot under latency.|Acknowledged final-state handoff and guarded finish; online unit and full shared-world round assertions. Critical.|\n|4|A cosmetic title-world attract flag shadowed the actual debug mode.|Semantic mode fields now override world fields; exact ten-second and exit assertions.|\n|5|New campaigns reused presentation event IDs, suppressing later effects/audio.|Presentation objects are disposed/recreated on each new world; campaign replay cleanup assertion.|\n|6|The final safe refill heart retained the danger palette.|Dock/refill heart now uses mint/cyan while airborne form remains dangerous; state-specific rendered coverage.|\n|7|A returning airborne Swirl was excluded from the final valid hit state.|Both flight and return count as airborne; simulation recovery/interception coverage.|\n|8|The native minimum1x scale cropped the fortress offscreen on phones.|Local engine fit patch plus nonnegative-offset/full-buffer-fit phone assertion and portrait image. Critical.|\n\n|9|WASD could override independent held mouse aim.|Mouse aiming now follows explicit aim ownership; combinedWASD/held-mouse regression.|\n|10|One-pad co-op could strand title/menu control onP2.|Title restores single-player device ownership; either scout’s controller can navigate pause. Actual stub regression coversP2pause/resume and title activation after quit. Critical.|\n|11|Sector-clear Pause/settings action opened no menu.|Transition now opens the real native Settings screen and returns to the transition; packaged campaign assertion.|\n|12|Custom touch pointers survived visibility loss.|Visibility and menu transitions clear ownership; actual concurrent-touch visibility regression.|\n\n## Actual online full-play conditions\n\nThe two-player host campaign completed under150ms latency,50ms jitter and5% loss, with matching victory/score on both copies. The dedicated-server campaign completed with its engine/render loop stopped and canvas hidden, with both remote players sending ordinary controls; added transport impairment was0for this full-play run. Its stateful test controller used read-only host awareness for planning, with no life, charge, damage or outcome mutations. Separate official dedicated-server stress checks used150/50/5, so progression proof and transport stress are not conflated. The sampled-controller suite also proves quantized100–150ms action holds and up to100ms input delay in the actual simulation.\n\n## Evidence interpretation\n\n`campaign-replays.json` records full Normal runs driven only by ordinary action frames. The packaged browser campaign also traverses actual scene updates, native transition actions, records and results. Corner-case fixtures jump to explicit states to test recovery; they do not replace full-play proof. Attract is invulnerable and is never used as balance evidence. The server test pilot is test infrastructure, separate from playable controls.\n\nPhysical controllers, a TV, touchscreen hardware, real iOS Safari and human listening were unavailable. Standard-mapping stubs,1920×1080 screenshots, browser touch emulation, WebKit and offline audio are accurately labeled substitutes. No FPS target or optimization budget was imposed. Longplay failure/progression and airborne-kill footage of the original were not independently watched; source instructions verify those relationships, and the research ledger records that limit.\n# Online verification evidence\n\nThe exact packaged root HTML is served by the test script through a temporary HTTP server bound to `127.0.0.1` on an OS-selected port. The script closes its browser/context and shuts down that server. The official pinned `vendor/hub/harness.html`, `bridge.js`, and `round.js` run unchanged.\n\n`tests/hub-browser.py` writes detailed JSON under `tests/artifacts/` and prints failures plus a final JSON count. It accepts `--official-only`, `--manual-only`, `--campaign-only`, `--server-only`, and `--campaign`; the root `npm test` wrapper invokes the appropriate coverage.\n\n## Proven scenarios\n\n- Official automatic conformance: one-seat shared-world session; two-seat co-op with 150 ms latency, 50 ms jitter and 5% loss; host departure; dedicated server with no player avatar. All four final runs passed every official check, with no page errors, client warnings, dialogs, popups, sockets, or sends/ticks after delivered end.\n- Actual two-player full campaign through ordinary actions: both browser pages emit the same controls as real devices through the live input adapter. Seed 1982; 150 ms latency, 50 ms jitter, 5% loss. The host completed all three sectors in 66.867 simulation seconds, with six core hits, two airborne interceptions, seven ordinary deaths, shared reserve recovery, and score 41727. Both clients ended with the same victory/score and only the host called finish. See `hub-campaign-standard.json`.\n- The final official/manual run reported **28 passed, 0 failed**. The non-host departure test verifies that the remaining scout continues and the departed body and pending respawn are removed. The hub may display a departed player last in its own results; it is absent from the game host’s active standings.\n- Short hidden-host simulation and the hub’s own over-20-second host-hidden void are tested through the official harness visibility bridge. The test waits for the delayed end to arrive before asserting no resume.\n\n- Actual dedicated-server full campaign: the host game engine/render loop is stopped and its canvas hidden, while its independent authoritative timer completes the same campaign. A read-only omniscient harness pilot chooses normal actions in each remote client; those controls traverse AI Hub and host validation. With no added latency/loss, actual combat seed 3249287277 completed in 60.25 simulation seconds, with six core hits, one airborne strike, five deaths, nine renewable refills, and score 41707 matching both clients and the server. No charge, reserve, phase, damage, or victory state is granted by the test. See `hub-campaign-server.json`. The separate official server conformance round still uses 150 ms latency, 50 ms jitter and 5% loss.\n- `tests/server-pilot.test.mjs` proves the stateful test controller under six sampled/quantized action traces, including 100–150 ms input holds and delays. It reads the world and emits the ordinary action shape. It does not mutate gameplay state.\n\n## Regression addressed\n\nDirect `finish()` initially could reach the hub before a final delayed network snapshot reached the other copy. The outcome-token acknowledgement in the normal input stream now guarantees a short final-state handoff before guarded finish, with a two-second fallback inside the five-second grace. This is covered by the mock suite and actual matching campaign outcome.\n\nThe original remote scripted pilot used a 0.1-second proportional steering time constant against a roughly 0.2–0.3-second network feedback loop, so a server-only round legitimately lost while oscillating around its strict cannon-alignment tolerance. This was a test-controller limitation, not an authority bypass or a granted victory. A stateful controller with damped steering, physical cannon tolerances, and committed evasive motion resolved the sampling instability. Its successful ordinary-action server playthrough supersedes those failed test-controller trials; no game balance or authority rule was relaxed.\n\n## Transport measurements\n\nDuring the first full combat proof, the host sent 676 packets in 66.867 simulation seconds, without rate drops. Maximum packet size was 5092 bytes (reliable topology recovery). All packets remained below the 16384-byte hard limit. 327 ordinary snapshots exceeded the advisory 1200-byte fragmentation size during busy combat; loss recovery and final convergence succeeded. The completed server campaign had a 5787-byte maximum reliable packet, zero send drops, and 239 ordinary snapshots over the advisory 1200-byte size. The shorter conformance intervals had no fragmented unreliable snapshots. These are measured protocol observations, not an FPS claim.\n\nMock tests additionally cover forged authority messages, impossible vectors, sender membership, stale packets, stale held-input expiry, cannon repeat/deduplication, countdown, host-local input, server ticking without rendering, phase-transition time-up, leave, exactly-once final submission, end cleanup, topology round trip, snapshot ordering, and false-send retry.\n-rw-r--r-- 1 micha micha 1.4M Nov  6 03:09 ../Yars-of-Ruin-Yars-Revenge-gpt-6.1-sol-ultra-20261106-022904.html\n-rw-r--r-- 1 micha micha 5.0K Nov  6 03:09 ../exit-interview.json\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_94","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('docs/testing.md');s=p.read_text().replace(' Critical.|\\\\n\\\\n|9|',' Critical.|\\\\n|9|');\nfor a,b in [('seeds1,1982,1106,4294967295','seeds 1, 1982, 1106, and 4294967295'),('a2.2s','a 2.2 s'),('minimum1x','minimum 1x'),('combinedWASD','combined WASD'),('onP2','on P2'),('coversP2pause/resume','covers P2 pause/resume'),('under150ms','under 150 ms'),('latency,50ms','latency, 50 ms'),('and5%','and 5%'),('was0for','was 0 for'),('quantized100–150ms','quantized 100–150 ms'),('to100ms','to 100 ms'),('stubs,1920','stubs, 1920'),('rendered96000','rendered 96,000'),('peak0.116842','peak 0.116842'),('RMS0.008605','RMS 0.008605'),('tailRMS0','tail RMS 0'),('at10%','at 10%'),('intervals,100','intervals, 100')]:s=s.replace(a,b)\np.write_text(s)\np=Path('../PROGRESS.md');p.write_text('''# Progress — Yars of Ruin\n\n## Deliverables\n- Root single HTML: \"'`Yars-of-Ruin-Yars-Revenge-gpt-6.1-sol-ultra-20261106-022904.html` (1.39 MiB, fully embedded).\n- Root `exit-interview.json` is complete, validated, and embedded in About / Credits.\n- All development files in the matching named subfolder; root PLAN/TASK remain intact.\n- `npm run build` regenerates root HTML and identical dev `index.html`; `npm test` is the quiet combined release suite.\n\n## Completed implementation\n- All three authored sectors and 1/2/3 heart strikes, final airborne-only interception with renewable dock.\n- Persistent stationary/moving/reflective shield cells, safe contact bites, charge, primary fire, physical rail cannon with owner/teammate danger, guided threats, static protection/weapon rules, fortress Swirl, wrapped geometry, exactly-once reserves/score/rewards.\n- Normal/Assist, discovered-sector practice, defeat/retry, replay, separate records and real ending/surviving swarm.\n- Custom articulated insects and dimensional native pixel fortress art, procedural animations, lighting, warnings/collapse, three-sector code-only music, effects and gibberish voices; 10% first-run music and actual master/music/effects/voice buses.\n- Native Shell title/pause/settings/rebinding, paginated how-to/credits, keyboard/mouse independent aim, standard gamepad glyphs, separate simultaneous local controls and safe pause-menu join; touch dual invisible sticks/contextual cannon with pointer cleanup; isolated 10-second attract loop.\n- Live dev sliders/reset, invulnerability/charge, collision overlays, seed, encounter/phase jumps, freeze/step/time scale/threat spawning, controller-operated cell painting, moving plates/refuge authoring, validated JSON import/export and presets.\n- AI Hub authoritative shared world with dedicated host, reliable cannon edges/ack/retry, compact recovery snapshots, membership/vector/timing validation, input expiry, leave/visibility/end/time-up ownership, exactly-once finish with acknowledged final state.\n\n## Pins and engine patch\n- my-3D2dge 0.89.1 / 8ecc2f62353761187bdfe226f327b7ea5d2eb691; Hub client 1.4.0 / 35a1047f2067b2af3de3943f0fe452f39105d869.\n- One local engine patch permits fractional fixed-arena fit below 1x. Upstream minimum 1x cropped the fortress on small phones. Exact patch and pristine checkout retained; all other engine code unchanged.\n- Session client remains pristine, first after charset. No runtime fetched assets/dependencies.\n\n## Verified before final combined run\n- Simulation: 35 passed (including four Normal seeds, local co-op, empty-shell final respawn/recovery, misses, authoring round-trip, exactly-once geometry/rewards).\n- Presentation: 5 passed; online mocks: 13 passed; sampled remote pilot: 6 passed; artifact: 16 passed; standalone browser: 55 passed.\n- Actual normal packaged solo campaign wins through native transition menus and ending/record assertions.\n- Official/manual Hub: 28 passed. Two-player full campaign under 150 ms latency / 50 ms jitter / 5% loss wins with matching score 41727. Hidden dedicated-server campaign, engine loop stopped, zero added impairment wins at 60.25 simulation seconds with matching score 41707; no gameplay grants. Server stress separately tested at 150/50/5. Full browser scripts now supply 43 Hub checks.\n- Audio offline proof: 96,000 finite samples; peak 0.116842, RMS 0.008605, silent tail. Actual bus/mute/saved-volume assertions pass.\n- 12 distinct defects found and fixed, 3 considered critical. Ledger in docs/testing.md. Test-fixture corrections excluded.\n- TV title/play/final heart/collapse/victory/credits and phone screenshots in tests/artifacts. Physical controller, real touch/iOS/TV and human listening unavailable; stand-ins documented honestly.\n\n## Active final gate\nThe final `npm run build && npm test` is running in exec session 93798 (started after all source formatting and final metadata). It runs every group against the actual shipped HTML. Poll it; fix any real failures, rebuild and rerun only affected coverage. No further feature work is planned. After success, record exact final count here and in docs/testing.md, verify identical HTML copies/interview parse, then hand in root HTML and exit interview with required four feedback sections in chat.\n\n## Ownership\nAll work confined to this workspace. No parent changes/commits. Browser/server processes are owned and closed by tests. No shared ports or process killing. All three subagents completed; root owns final verification.\n'\"''')\nPY\nls -l tests/artifacts/browser-summary.json tests/artifacts/hub-browser-summary.json tests/artifacts/hub-1-standard.json\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
